+## Current activation (momentum cost-sensitivity under the existing cost model — 44861598276 / agent 02)
+
+Campaign task R-001 (HIGHER_ORDER_OBJECTIVE, slot 02) asked whether the
+short-horizon momentum edge survives conservative transaction-cost stress on the
+collected real-data universe. Bounded, predeclared cost grid: zero (repository
+default), realistic (the repository's existing realistic model: $2/trade +
+$0.003/share, 2c fixed + 5 bps proportional slippage, as in
+`examples/ma_crossover_real_data.py`), and stress (2x realistic). Lookbacks
+3/5/10, 10-asset universe, walk-forward per volatility segment
+(train=252d/test=84d/warmup=60d/overlap=60d, 4 blocks, min 400 bars/segment),
+each cost level compared with a coin-flip null matched to the same costs.
+New check `research/checks/momentum_cost_sensitivity.py`, independent verifier
+`research/checks/verify_momentum_cost_sensitivity.py`, artifact
+`state/check_artifacts/momentum_cost_sensitivity_results.json`.
+
+Results (seed 42, dataset yf-ohlcv-universe-2009-to-2026-10-03):
+
+- Candidate segment medians are essentially cost-invariant. At lookback 5,
+  9/10 assets have strictly positive segment medians at zero, realistic, and
+  2x stress (verdict-independent); the only exception is NVDA (one negative
+  segment, which becomes more negative with cost: -0.038 -> -0.064 -> -0.088).
+- Verdict-based positive-edge counts at lookback 5 are 6/10 at all three cost
+  levels; lookback-ROBUST (>=2 lookbacks with a positive edge) is 6/10 at all
+  three levels; the predeclared aggregate class is COST_ROBUST.
+- Verdict flips occur without candidate degradation: MSFT's medians are
+  unchanged [0.052, 0.072] but its verdict flips REGIME_STABLE ->
+  REGIME_DEPENDENT at realistic cost, and JPM's unchanged [0.113, 0.044, 0.083]
+  flips REGIME_DEPENDENT -> REGIME_STABLE. These are null-dispersion artifacts:
+  the matched coin-flip null's dispersion changes with the cost level, and the
+  REGIME_STABLE/REGIME_DEPENDENT label depends on it. The verdict-independent
+  median view is the more reliable reading.
+- Cost magnitude is small under this model: full-sample AMZN realistic
+  round-trip cost is $135,860 on $1M initial capital over 2009-2026, and the
+  zero->realistic total-return difference is 0.019 (1.9% of initial), because
+  the engine adds slippage to the fill price for both buys and sells (symmetric,
+  not adverse), so slippage largely cancels over round trips and commission
+  dominates. This makes the existing cost model optimistic for a high-turnover
+  strategy; the 2x stress level is still mild in economic terms.
+
+Verification: `python3 research/checks/momentum_cost_sensitivity.py` exits 0
+(manifest 10/10 OK, preflight PASSED, leakage PASS at all three cost levels,
+determinism r1==r2); `python3 research/checks/verify_momentum_cost_sensitivity.py`
+exits 0 with Gate 1 fresh walk_forward + noise_benchmark MATCH on AMZN/JPM for
+all 3 lookbacks x 3 cost levels, Gate 2 zero-cost column MATCH vs
+`momentum_lookback_sweep_results.json` on all 10 tickers x 3 lookbacks, Gate 3
+classification and median-positive counts reproduce, cost-grid integrity MATCH,
+determinism identical; `python3 -m unittest discover -s tests -v` -> 168 tests,
+all passing.
+
+Assessment: within the repository's existing cost model, the momentum edge is
+not eliminated by realistic or 2x stress costs (aggregate COST_ROBUST;
+candidate medians cost-invariant). This resolves the recorded cost-robustness
+qualification for that model. It does NOT settle adverse-slippage cost
+robustness: the engine's symmetric slippage makes the test optimistic, and an
+adverse-slippage cost model is the next decision-relevant check. The momentum
+candidate remains admitted as candidate positive evidence with this
+qualification; no negative result overturns it.
+

